# Deliberate binary breaks in 1.11.0, relative to 1.10.0. See the release note in README.md.
#
# Issue #67: randomised iteration was not reproducible, because there were two sources of
# randomness and they disagreed about where the Random came from. VertexMap.randomAdjacencies
# took the caller's; AbstractBag.iterator picked up an ambient, process-global, unseeded
# Bag.random through an import, ignoring any Random the caller had in scope. So a seed pinned
# at a call site did real work on one path and nothing at all on the other, with no way to
# tell which you were getting. Nothing was flaky, but the bag path was unreproducible BY
# CONSTRUCTION: an order-dependent failure there could not have been reproduced by pinning a
# seed, because the seed that mattered was private, global, and set from the clock.
#
# The fix, and why each break follows from it:
#
#   - Bag.iterator now returns insertion order. It is also the IterableOnce iterator, so it
#     cannot take a Random; shuffling moves to the new Bag.randomIterator(using Random), which
#     always uses the caller's generator. This also keeps size, isEmpty and contains off any
#     PRNG. Unordered gains the same pair, so everything built on a bag randomises alike.
#     => ReversedMissingMethodProblem on Bag.randomIterator and Unordered.randomIterator
#        (new abstract members on published traits).
#
#   - The ambient Bag.random is deleted outright, so there is no longer an unreachable
#     generator anyone can pick up by accident.
#     => DirectMissingMethodProblem on Bag.random.
#
#   - filteredAdjacencies now takes (using random: Random = Random()) and calls randomIterator,
#     so both adjacency paths draw from the same place. The default is only consulted when the
#     caller has no given in scope, so a caller's seed always wins — which is the difference
#     from the old ambient value, and the whole point.
#     => DirectMissingMethodProblem on the old arity in Traversable, AbstractGraph and
#        VertexMap, plus ReversedMissingMethodProblem on the new one in Traversable.
#
#   - VertexMap.neighboursGiven took its randomness from the same deleted import, so it now
#     takes the generator explicitly — which is what Traversable.graphNeighbours had always
#     required.
#     => DirectMissingMethodProblem on VertexMap.neighboursGiven.
#
# Worth recording, because it contradicts the issue: #67 also asked for the removal of a dead
# `using @unused random: Random` on Kosaraju.stronglyConnectedComponents. After this change
# that parameter is no longer dead — it reaches filteredAdjacencies down the chain — and the
# same proved true of the five other @unused random parameters, in ShortestPaths and Graph.
# All six annotations are removed and every parameter kept. The dead parameter was a symptom
# of the defect, not a separate one, so no further break was needed for it.
#
# Under PVP, 1.10.x -> 1.11.0 is a major bump, so these breaks are permitted. This file retires
# itself: once previousRelease moves past 1.10.0, MiMa stops consulting it.
#
# The lines below are MiMa's own suggestions from the failing run, not hand-written.

ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.AbstractGraph.filteredAdjacencies(scala.Function1,java.lang.Object)scala.collection.Iterator")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ReversedMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Bag.randomIterator(scala.util.Random)scala.collection.Iterator")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Bag.random()scala.util.Random")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Traversable.filteredAdjacencies(scala.Function1,java.lang.Object)scala.collection.Iterator")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ReversedMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Traversable.filteredAdjacencies(scala.Function1,java.lang.Object,scala.util.Random)scala.collection.Iterator")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ReversedMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Unordered.randomIterator(scala.util.Random)scala.collection.Iterator")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap.filteredAdjacencies(scala.Function1,java.lang.Object)scala.collection.Iterator")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap.neighboursGiven()com.phasmidsoftware.visitor.core.Neighbours")

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #67, second round: getConnexions could not be seeded either.
#
# Found by a test rather than by reading: pinning a seed in TraversableSpec did not make
# "ProtoConnexions.create" deterministic, because `Traversable.getConnexions` and
# `Connexions.create` take no Random and so could not forward one. The caller's given stopped
# at their signatures and the traversal inside used a fresh generator. Both now take
# `using Random = Random()`. Without this the first round of the fix was incomplete: it made
# the bag path seedable and left an entry point that still was not.
# => DirectMissingMethodProblem on getConnexions in Traversable, AbstractGraph and VertexMap,
#    and on Connexions.create.

ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.AbstractGraph.getConnexions(java.lang.Object)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Connexions")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Traversable.getConnexions(java.lang.Object)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Connexions")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap.getConnexions(java.lang.Object)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Connexions")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Connexions.create(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.Traversable,java.lang.Object)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Connexions")

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #71: TraversalResult's vertex/edge duality, where only one half was ever populated.
#
# `edgeTraverse` was on the common trait, so the vertex-keyed branch had to implement it and
# did so by throwing — a method on a public type whose only behaviour was to throw, which the
# type system nonetheless advertised. The edge-keyed branch is NOT dead, contrary to what the
# issue supposed: `TraversalResult.edgeTraversal` populates it and `TopologicalSortSpec` reads
# it. So the fix is the issue's second option, splitting rather than deleting: `edgeTraverse`
# now lives only on `AbstractEdgeTraversalResult`, and `edgeTraversal` returns that type rather
# than the general one, so the method is still reachable where it means something.
# => DirectMissingMethodProblem on edgeTraverse in the trait and the vertex branch, and
#    IncompatibleResultTypeProblem on edgeTraversal.

ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.TraversalResult.edgeTraverse(Int)java.lang.Object")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.AbstractVertexTraversalResult.edgeTraverse(Int)java.lang.Object")
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleResultTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.TraversalResult.edgeTraversal(scala.Function1,com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.EdgeTraversable)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.TraversalResult")

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #69 and #70: two public case classes that were reachable but inert.
#
# `EdgeList` and `VertexPairList` each had exactly one consumer in main, and each consumer did
# nothing but unwrap the wrapper: `VertexMap.addEdges` read `.edges` and folded, and
# `VertexMap.createFromVertexPairList` read `.pairs` and called `addVertexPairs`, which already
# took the raw sequence. Their one real method, `triplets`, was called nowhere but the spec
# that exists to test it. So a caller holding a sequence had to box it purely to have it
# unboxed again.
#
# Both move to the test tree rather than being deleted, which keeps them as test implementors
# of `SerializableGraph`, `Edges` and `Pairs` — coverage that would otherwise go with them.
# `addEdges` now takes `Seq[Edge[V, E]]`, which also retires its `Z` parameter: `Z` existed
# only to type `EdgeList.triplets` and never appeared in the method's body.
# `createFromVertexPairList` goes entirely, being `addVertexPairs` on an empty map.
#
# `Triplets` stays in main, moving from `EdgeList.scala` to its own file: it is the
# `SerializableGraph` implementor that main actually uses, via `VertexMap.createFromTriplets`.
# => MissingClassProblem on both case classes and their companions, IncompatibleMethTypeProblem
#    on addEdges, DirectMissingMethodProblem on createFromVertexPairList.

ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.EdgeList")
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.EdgeList$")
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexPairList")
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexPairList$")
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleMethTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap.addEdges(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.EdgeList)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap.createFromVertexPairList(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexPairList)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.core.VertexMap")

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Collapsing the two unweighted traversal APIs into one.
#
# Gryphon had two implementations of one operation — run a DFS or BFS, map each visited vertex,
# return a TraversalResult. `Traversable.vertexMappedTraversalDfs`/`Bfs` carried one copy and
# had callers only in tests; `DFSTraversal`/`BFSTraversal` carried the other and had no callers
# at all, in main, in the specs, or in the Java façade. Giving DFS and BFS a shared base in the
# first 1.11.0 PR deduplicated the pair that nothing called without touching the overlap, which
# is the duplication that actually mattered.
#
# The difference between the two was `fulfill`, so that moves into `UnweightedTraversal`, which
# becomes `UnweightedTraversal[V, T](fulfill: V => T)`. `DFSTraversal` and `BFSTraversal` take
# it too, with `DFSTraversal[V]()` kept as the identity case through a companion `apply`. The
# `Traversable` methods now delegate, which also gives those two classes their first real
# caller. `Traversable.traversalResult` goes with the bodies that used it: the unwrapping of a
# journal into a `VertexTraversalResult` now happens once, in `UnweightedTraversal.run`.
#
# The case classes gained a parameter, so their synthetic members change shape.
# => DirectMissingMethodProblem on the no-arg constructors and copy, IncompatibleResultTypeProblem
#    on unapply. The `Traversable` methods keep their signatures, so callers of those are
#    unaffected.

ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.DFSTraversal.this()Unit")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.DFSTraversal.copy()com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.DFSTraversal")
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleResultTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.DFSTraversal.unapply(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.DFSTraversal)Boolean")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.BFSTraversal.this()Unit")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.BFSTraversal.copy()com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.BFSTraversal")
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleResultTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.BFSTraversal.unapply(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.BFSTraversal)Boolean")

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #56: an attributeless connection is no longer admitted into an attributed graph.
#
# A VertexPair — a connection carrying nothing — is a perfectly good thing, and is all a graph
# needs unless its edges have an attribute such as a weight. The defect was what happened when
# one was admitted into a graph whose edges are supposed to carry an E: both flavours accepted
# it and then lied about the type. DirectedGraph built a VertexPair and later cast
# OrderedEdge(...), a DirectedEdge[V, Unit], to DirectedEdge[V, E]; UndirectedGraph built
# UndirectedEdge(None, ...), putting None in a field typed E. Measured, not inferred: reading
# `.attribute` on either, in a Double-weighted graph, throws ClassCastException at the use site.
#
# Note the issue says the undirected side was fixed in 1.8.0. What 1.8.0 fixed was a different
# symptom — the adjacency counting in `degree` but not in `edges` or `M`. Both sides were still
# unsound, the undirected one laundering its cast through an @unchecked pattern instead.
#
# The new AbsentAttribute type class answers the question the construction code could not: is E
# a type for which "no attribute" is a legitimate value? For Unit it is, and the value is ().
# For anything else it is not, and fromTriplets now returns a Failure naming the offending
# triplet rather than building a graph that detonates later. triplesToTryGraph and
# GraphBuilder's factories therefore require the evidence; GraphBuilder needs it as a context
# bound rather than resolving it internally, because the function value it builds would
# otherwise capture the fallback instance with E still abstract.
#
# This withdraws a capability: a graph can no longer MIX attributed and attributeless edges.
# GraphPropertiesSpec had a test for that, added with the 1.8.0 fix, which passed only because
# the attributeless edge was given an attribute of the wrong type. It is now a test that the
# construction is refused. Robin took that decision deliberately (2026-11-23); the alternative
# was to make attributes Option[E] throughout, which is a far larger change.
# => DirectedGraph.triplesToTryGraph, UndirectedGraph.triplesToTryGraph,
#    AbstractAdjunctGraph.fromTriplets, GraphBuilder.undirected and GraphBuilder.directed all
#    gain a context parameter, so their erased signatures change.

ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.AbstractAdjunctGraph.fromTriplets(scala.Function1,scala.Function1,scala.Function3,scala.Function1,scala.Function1,scala.collection.immutable.Seq)scala.util.Try")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.DirectedGraph.triplesToTryGraph(scala.Function1,scala.collection.immutable.Seq)scala.util.Try")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.UndirectedGraph.triplesToTryGraph(scala.Function1,scala.collection.immutable.Seq)scala.util.Try")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.builder.GraphBuilder.undirected(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.parse.Parseable,com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.parse.Parseable)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.builder.GraphBuilder")
ProblemFilters.exclude[DirectMissingMethodProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.builder.GraphBuilder.directed(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.parse.Parseable,com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.parse.Parseable)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.builder.GraphBuilder")
